Catalytic converters are now included in perhaps three quarters of all new vehicles produced worldwide, although the PGM loading in these varies since many local models are not yet required to meet the more stringent US and European emission standards.  In recent years, autocatalysts have typically accounted for around 50% of all gross palladium demand.  However, in 2011 this proportion jumped to 59% on the back of the ongoing recovery in auto sales and production. 

Autocatalysts are constructed by applying a coating of palladium, platinum and rhodium onto a ceramic honeycomb.  Total PGM loadings can vary from 5 to 25 grams per vehicle. Recently, we calculate that average palladium use has fluctuated around 3.1 grams (0.10 oz) per catalyst fitted in the developing world, 2.3-2.6 grams (0.075-0.085 oz) per catalyst fitted in Japan, 2.6-3.0 grams (0.085-0.095 oz) per catalyst fitted in Western Europe and around 3.4 grams (0.11 oz) per catalyst fitted in North America.  Today’s global average is estimated to be around 3.1 grams (0.1 oz). 

